# Writing Plans Another Agent Will Execute

Plans are executed literally by models that satisfice under friction. Every
requirement that depends on the executor's judgment will degrade. Write plans so
drift fails **mechanically**, not socially.

**This rule is always applied.** Any time you produce, revise, or review an
implementation plan in this repo, you must follow it and emit the **Compliance
Manifest** below — do not wait to be asked. The same principle that governs
executors governs plan-authoring and plan-review themselves: "did this plan
follow the rules?" is a judgment task that samples, so it is replaced here by a
mechanical, itemized manifest that a reviewer ticks line-by-line.

## The three failure modes to design against

1. **Assertion weakening** — when a comparison fails, the executor loosens it
   (sorts both sides, switches to set-compare, drops a field) instead of finding
   the root cause.
   - For every comparison, state explicitly whether **order, count, and
     exactness** matter.
   - Include an anti-weakening clause verbatim: *"If an assertion fails, the bug
     is elsewhere — never loosen the assertion to pass. Fix the root cause or
     record it as a suspected regression."*

2. **List sampling** — a prose enumeration of N requirements gets implemented as
   ~70% of N.
   - Never enumerate requirements in a sentence. Use an itemized checklist with
     an explicit count: *"all 13 of the following — this table is exhaustive."*
   - Where possible, make the count **self-enforcing**: have the test iterate a
     collected list and assert its length, so under-implementation fails CI
     instead of relying on diligence.

3. **Quantifier erosion** — "every X" silently becomes "the first X."
   - Replace universal quantifiers with mechanically checkable criteria: iterate
     `querySelectorAll(...)` / registry values and assert non-trivial coverage,
     or fail loudly when the iterated set is empty.

## Patterns that work (include them)

- **Sanctioned escape hatches.** Executors will hit blockers. Give exactly one
  auditable way out: an explicit, initially-empty allowlist constant
  (`EXPECTED_MISSING`, `SWEEP_EXEMPT`) where every entry requires a justifying
  comment. This prevents invented silent skips.
- **Bounded blast radius.** Name what may NOT be modified to make tests pass
  (e.g. *"do not touch `backgrounds.js` or `appCommands.js` — these tests
  DESCRIBE current behavior"*) and what to do instead (record as a suspected
  regression in the final summary).
- **File-specific pitfall notes.** Concrete warnings ("jsdom returns zeros from
  `getBoundingClientRect()`, so stub the measurement seam in `Canvas.jsx` tests";
  "reset the Zustand store in `beforeEach` — it is a module singleton") are the
  most-followed part of any plan. Abstract advice is the least.
- **Exact paths, no improvisation.** List every file to create/touch with full
  paths; state "do not create new test directories."
- **Per-todo verification.** Each todo names the command that proves it done
  (`npx vitest run src/utils/__tests__/slideParser.test.ts`), plus the repo-wide
  gate at the end (`npm run gate`).
- **Snapshot policy inline.** If snapshots are involved, state the update policy
  in the plan AND require it as a header comment in the test file (never blanket
  `vitest -u`; name intentional refreshes in the commit message).
- **Preconditions for conditional UI.** Controls gated on state are the top
  source of false "missing control" failures — the formatting toolbar only
  renders with a selected text box; media controls depend on section type;
  output settings depend on detected displays. For each assertion, the plan
  states the seed state needed to make the target reachable, or instructs the
  executor to read the gate condition in the component before concluding a
  control is absent.
- **Structural floor under every snapshot.** Pair each snapshot with a cheap
  invariant asserted in the same test, so an empty or garbage baseline cannot be
  committed as "passing." State explicitly: an empty artifact means the harness
  failed — fix the harness, never the snapshot.
- **IPC contract pinning.** If the plan touches any `ipcMain` / `ipcRenderer`
  channel, it must name the channel and require a test asserting the exact
  payload shape on both sides. This seam is this app's highest-risk surface.
- **Manual verification steps.** This is a desktop app. If the change is
  user-facing, the plan's final todo lists the exact click-path to verify in a
  running window (`npm run dev`), including which display configuration to test
  when output assignment is involved.
- **Required findings report.** The plan's final todo mandates a structured
  summary: suspected pre-existing regressions, every allowlist/exemption entry
  added (with justification), every intentional snapshot refresh, and any
  discovered behavior the plan didn't anticipate. Escape hatches are only
  auditable if their use must be reported.

## Required output: the Compliance Manifest

Every plan ends with a **Compliance Manifest** — the mechanism that makes rule
compliance fail loudly at authoring time instead of being rediscovered after the
work is done. A plan without a complete manifest is not finished.

For each project rule whose scope the plan's work touches, copy that rule's
**"Plan compliance checklist"** into the manifest and give **every** item a
disposition — no item may be blank:

- the **todo number / plan section** that upholds it, or
- `N/A — <one-line reason>` (the reason is mandatory; a bare `N/A` is a blank).

Which checklists apply:

- **This rule** — *always*. Confirm the three failure modes are designed against
  and each "Patterns that work" item is used or `N/A`.
- **Testing** — *always* (every plan changes, or should change, tests):
  `testing-standards.mdc` → "Plan compliance checklist" (all 10 items).

The manifest is the itemized, counted checklist itself (the anti-list-sampling
rule, applied to the plan) — never a prose paragraph claiming "this plan follows
the rules." If a rule later gains an item, the manifest's count is wrong and the
gap is visible; that is the point.

## Reviewer checklist (for auditing an implementation against its plan)

This checklist is the mechanical backing of the **Self-Review Report** gate
defined in `AI_OPERATING_MANUAL.md` §3.2. Run it in a **fresh context — you did
not write this code** — and end with an explicit **PASS / FAIL verdict** naming
the top issues the architect must check locally. No change merges without a clean
pass plus an architect-run completion gate (Operating Manual Non-Negotiable #2).

- **First, audit the Compliance Manifest.** A missing manifest is an automatic
  fail — send it back. For every manifest item, open the cited todo/section and
  confirm it actually upholds the item; reject any blank entry or any `N/A`
  without a justifying reason. Re-derive which checklists *should* apply from the
  plan's file list and confirm none was omitted.
- Diff every "all/every/each" claim in the plan against the implementation —
  count, don't trust.
- Search the tests for `.sort()`, set-compares, `toContain` where the plan
  implied exact equality — each is a candidate weakened assertion.
- Verify allowlists are empty or every entry has a justifying comment.
- Confirm no out-of-scope source files changed (`git diff --stat` against the
  plan's file list); flag any, even if justified, in the review summary.
- Confirm every touched IPC channel has a payload-shape test on both sides.
- Re-run the plan's gate command yourself; never accept a reported result.
